// ============================================================================
// glossary.z  --  what the words in unified.z mean
// ============================================================================
//
// Read this before unified.z. It defines no syntax; it only names things.
//
// ============================================================================
// HOW TO READ A DECLARATION
// ============================================================================
//
//     Name <compile-time args> (runtime args) -> projection { body }
//     ^     ^                  ^                ^             ^
//     |     |                  |                |             the BLOCK
//     |     |                  |                what ESCAPES the block
//     |     |                  known at run time
//     |     known at compile time
//     the binding
//
// Every construct in the language is that one line. What kind of thing you get
// is decided entirely by which parts you write.
//
//
// ============================================================================
// CORE VOCABULARY
// ============================================================================
//
// BLOCK
//     The `{ ... }` of a declaration, and everything named inside it. This is
//     the only container in the language. A struct is a block, a module is a
//     block, a function body is a block, an interface is a block, a sum is a
//     block, a closure is a block.
//
//     "The block" is what `self` always refers to.
//
// MEMBER
//     A name inside a block. Three kinds, told apart by the binder:
//
//         name :: v      compile-time member.  Costs 0 bytes. Methods and
//                        compile-time constants are these.
//         name := v      runtime member.       A field; occupies storage.
//         name : T       a HOLE -- see below.  No binder at all.
//
// BINDER
//     `::` or `:=`. The thing between a name and its value. `:` is NOT a
//     binder -- it is type ascription and nothing else, so `x : u32 := 3`
//     reads "x, of type u32, bound at run time to 3".
//
//     `=` is not a binder either. It assigns: mutates an existing binding,
//     fills a declared hole, gives a parameter a default, or passes a
//     compile-time argument by name. It NEVER creates a name.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH BINDER DO I WRITE?  --  the question this glossary exists for
//
//     Three spellings for what looks like the same act. They are distinguished
//     by ONE question: DOES THIS NAME ALREADY EXIST?
//
//         format: (v: T, w: ptr<Writer>) -> ()   declare a hole. Name exists,
//                                                value does not. No binder.
//         format :: (v, w) -> () { ... }         CREATE the name and supply it.
//         format = (v, w) -> () { ... }          FILL a hole declared
//                                                elsewhere. Creates nothing.
//
//     The PROJECTION tells you which case you are in, every time:
//
//         -> self        you are building a fresh block. Nothing exists yet,
//                        so every member is `::` or `:=`.
//         -> SomeType    you are building a value of a type that ALREADY HAS
//                        HOLES. Members matching those holes are `=`.
//
//     So in `List :: <T, M> (mem: M) -> self`, `push :: (&self, ...)` creates
//     a member. In `spaced :: -> Formattable<Json>`, `format = (v, w) ...`
//     fills the hole `Formattable` declared.
//
//     Mixing is normal. A helper that is NOT one of the type's holes is a
//     fresh member, written `::`, and it does not escape:
//
//         spaced :: -> Formattable<Json> {
//             format = (v, w) -> () { ... self.indent(w) ... }   // fills
//             indent :: (w: ptr<Writer>) -> () { ... }           // creates
//         }
//
// HOLE
//     A member with a type and NO binder: `alloc: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr`.
//     Something that is declared but not supplied.
//
//     A hole is the single mechanism behind interfaces AND sum types. What it
//     means depends on the projection -- see PROJECTION.
//
// PROJECTION
//     What comes after `->`. It says what escapes the block, and it is what
//     makes one declaration form able to be five things:
//
//         -> ()        nothing escapes                    a procedure
//         -> T         one value escapes                  a function
//         -> self      all of the block escapes           struct / module
//         -> {a, b}    only these members escape          multiple return,
//                                                         and visibility
//         -> oneof     exactly one member escapes         a sum
//
//     The projection also decides how HOLES are read:
//
//         -> self    ALL holes must be filled to build a value. A block with
//                    an unfilled hole cannot be instantiated, which is the
//                    entire definition of "interface".
//         -> oneof   EXACTLY ONE hole is filled. That is a sum.
//
// ABSTRACT
//     Not a keyword. A `-> self` block with at least one unfilled hole. You
//     cannot make one, so you can only use it as a bound or behind a pointer.
//     Filling every hole makes it an ordinary value.
//
//
// ============================================================================
// THE TWO KINDS OF ABSTRACTION
// ============================================================================
//
// This is the pair of words that causes the most confusion, so at length.
// Both are "a block with unfilled holes". They differ in one thing: whether
// the holes take `self`.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SHAPE   --  every hole takes self
//
//     Writer :: -> self {
//         write_bytes: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> ()
//     }
//
//     A description of what a TYPE CAN DO. Any type with matching members
//     satisfies it, structurally, with no registration and no declaration of
//     intent. The answer is a property of the object: a File writes one way, a
//     Socket writes another, and neither has a second opinion about it.
//
//     Nearest thing in other languages: a Go interface, a C++20 concept,
//     Rust's `dyn Trait`.
//
//     Dispatch: `w.write_bytes(...)` -- the receiver decides.
//     Erasure:  `ptr<Writer>` is a fat pointer (data + vtable).
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// POLICY   --  no hole takes self
//
//     Ord :: <T> -> self {
//         compare: (a: T, b: T) -> i32
//     }
//
//     A description of HOW TO TREAT A TYPE FOR SOME PURPOSE. It is not
//     attached to the type; it is a free-standing value the caller supplies.
//     Several can exist for the same type, which is the entire point.
//
//     Nearest thing in other languages: Java's `Comparator<T>`, C++'s
//     `std::less<T>` or an allocator template argument, Python's `key=` to
//     `sorted()`, a Haskell typeclass instance.
//
//     Dispatch: `O.compare(a, b)` -- the CALL SITE decides, by choosing O.
//     Defaulting: written anonymously, it goes in the solver POOL and is
//     found automatically. Written with a name, it is reachable only by hand.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH ONE AM I WRITING?
//
//     Ask: can there legitimately be TWO of these for one type?
//
//         Two orderings of u8?          Yes -- ascending, descending.
//                                       -> policy
//         Two ways for an Arena to
//         allocate?                     No.
//                                       -> shape
//
//     Or equivalently: is the behavior a PROPERTY OF THE THING, or a CHOICE
//     THE CALLER MAKES ABOUT THE THING? Writing is a property of a file.
//     Case-insensitive comparison is a choice you make about a string.
//
//     Mixing the two in one block is an error, not a third kind of thing.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW YOU PROVIDE THE MEMBERS, AND WHY IT IS SPELLED TWO WAYS
//
//     This is the most common source of confusion in the language, and it is
//     not arbitrary -- it is the structural/nominal difference, showing up in
//     the binder.
//
//         Heap :: () -> self {
//             alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr { c_malloc(n) }      // creates
//         }
//
//         spaced :: -> Formattable<Json> {
//             format = (v, w) -> () { ... }                        // fills
//         }
//
//     Both look like "provide an implementation", and they are spelled
//     differently because they are not the same act.
//
//     `alloc` IS a hole in Allocator -- but Heap never says `-> Allocator`. It
//     says `-> self` and satisfies the shape STRUCTURALLY, by its members
//     happening to match. There is no hole in scope, so `alloc` is a fresh
//     member, written `::`.
//
//     A policy instance is the opposite. It MUST name its type in the
//     projection, because that is how the solver finds it. So the type's holes
//     are in scope, and `format` fills one, written `=`.
//
//                          SHAPE                     POLICY
//         satisfied by     structure -- the member   naming the type in the
//                          names match               projection
//         members written  `::`  -- creating         `=`   -- filling
//         checked against  nothing; any shape it     the named type's holes,
//                          satisfies is incidental   signature by signature
//
//     Reading the binder therefore tells you something real. `::` means "fresh
//     member; whatever shape it happens to satisfy is a coincidence". `=`
//     means "honouring a contract that exists elsewhere, and the compiler is
//     checking the signature against it".
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY "POLICY" AND NOT "DICTIONARY"
//
//     Earlier drafts called this a DICTIONARY, from GHC's implementation of
//     Haskell typeclasses: a constraint compiles into an extra hidden
//     argument, a record of the class's functions, which that compiler calls a
//     dictionary. Accurate, but jargon from one compiler's internals -- and to
//     everyone else, and to `Map` in json.z in this repo, a dictionary is a
//     HASH MAP. Two meanings, one codebase.
//
//     POLICY is from C++ policy-based design and evokes the right thing: a
//     choice plugged in by the caller. One wrinkle to know about -- in C++ the
//     allocator is THE canonical policy, and here `Allocator` is a SHAPE. The
//     test below, not the C++ analogy, decides which you are writing.
//
//     Also considered: STRATEGY (the GoF pattern; a Comparator literally is
//     one, and it is the most widely recognised -- but it connotes a runtime
//     object, which these are not) and WITNESS (from type theory, the most
//     precise and the most obscure; it evokes proof rather than choice, and
//     choice is the defining feature).
//
//     `shape` has no such problem and stays.
//
//     One casualty of the same Haskell vocabulary remains: INSTANCE, below,
//     which already means "an instance of a struct" to everyone.
//
//
// ============================================================================
// DISPATCH VOCABULARY
// ============================================================================
//
// SOLVER
//     The compile-time search that fills a `= ?` parameter. When you write
//     `sort(&xs)` and sort wants an `O: Ord<T> = ?`, nothing at the call site
//     mentions O, so the solver goes and finds one.
//
// POOL
//     What the solver searches: every ANONYMOUS declaration. `:: -> Ord<u8>`
//     with no name on the left is in the pool. `reverse_u8 :: -> Ord<u8>` is
//     not, and can only be reached by writing its name.
//
// INSTANCE
//     One filled-in policy. `reverse_u8` is an instance of `Ord<u8>`.
//     (Note the collision with the everyday sense of "an instance of a
//     struct". Another casualty of the Haskell vocabulary.)
//
// MONOMORPHISE
//     Compile a separate copy of a generic per concrete type. `List<u8>(mem)`
//     with `mem: Heap` produces code specific to Heap: calls are direct, the
//     allocator field is 0 bytes. The cost is that `List<u8, Heap>` and
//     `List<u8, Arena>` are different types that cannot share an array.
//
// ERASE
//     The opposite: store the answer with the data instead of in the type.
//     `boxed : ptr<Allocator> := &arena` erases. Now `List<u8, ptr<Allocator>>`
//     is one type whatever allocator is inside, and calls are indirect.
//
// FAT POINTER
//     What `ptr<Shape>` is: two words, the data pointer and a vtable pointer.
//     Built at the moment of coercion, and only there.
//
// VTABLE
//     The table of function pointers inside a fat pointer, one slot per hole.
//
// RECEIVER
//     The `self` or `&self` first parameter of a shape's member. The value the
//     method was called on.
//
// INTRINSIC / EXTRINSIC
//     Where a callable is declared, which decides whether it can satisfy a
//     shape:
//
//         INTRINSIC   declared inside the block. Counts for shape matching.
//                     `push`, `alloc`, `write_bytes`.
//         EXTRINSIC   a free function reached through UFCS. Convenience only,
//                     NEVER satisfies a shape. `make`, `drop`, `write`.
//
//     The second half matters: UFCS finds free functions by ordinary lookup in
//     the CALLER's scope, so if extrinsics counted, "does Heap satisfy
//     Allocator" would depend on what happened to be imported.
//
// UFCS
//     Uniform Function Call Syntax. `a.f(x)` falls back to `f(a, x)` when `a`
//     has no member `f`. Sugar for calling, and nothing else.
//
//
// ============================================================================
// KEYWORDS  --  the entire budget
// ============================================================================
//
//     self        "the block". The only new word in the declaration form.
//                 -> self             a projection
//                 self, &self         a receiver
//                 ptr<self>           what &self desugars to
//                 -> Result<self, E>  a projection may wrap it
//
//     oneof       the sum projection
//     match, _    destructuring; `_` is a hole in any binding position
//     try         propagate the `err` arm of a Result
//     defer       run at scope exit
//     if while for loop return continue break
//
//
// ============================================================================
// WORDS THAT ARE NOT IN THE LANGUAGE, AND WHAT REPLACES THEM
// ============================================================================
//
//     struct      a `-> self` block. Params are the fields.
//     module      a `-> self` block with no runtime params.
//     enum        a `-> oneof` block.
//     union       a `-> oneof` block. (Tagged. There is no untagged one.)
//     interface   a `-> self` block with unfilled holes.
//     trait       same. Split into SHAPE and POLICY -- see above.
//     abstract    a block with an unfilled hole already is.
//     impl        an ordinary declaration whose projection is the shape.
//     class       -- 
//     operator    a member with a symbolic name: `[]`, `()`.
//     pub         the projection is the export list: `-> {a, b}`.
//     dyn         `ptr<Shape>`.
//     import      an ordinary compile-time binding of an ordinary function:
//                 `std :: import("std")`.
//     const       -- nothing yet. `::` gives immutable BINDINGS; there is no
//                 story for immutable POINTEES. Open.
//
//
// ============================================================================
// THE THREE-LINE VERSION
// ============================================================================
//
//     A block is the only container.
//     A hole is a member you did not supply.
//     The projection says what escapes, and how the holes are read.
//
// Everything else is a consequence.
// ============================================================================
